{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-36219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{3x+y}{\\sqrt{6xy+{y}^{2}}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-36219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,2,4)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,2) & =\\frac{3(1)+(2)}{\\sqrt{6(1)(2)+(2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-4 & =\\frac{5}{4}(y-2) \\\\ z & =\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-36219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ae308f1e0db306be55d9e03c6986121dbe1449a1f71b70df037c9dbd3a1fcad8"} {"question" "q-36219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-36219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{4}{5}y-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{2}y-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=-\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-36219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-132587" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2xy+2$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-132587" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,2,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)} & =2(1)(2)+2 \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)}(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =6(x-1) \\\\ z & =6x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=6x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-132587" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ae308f1e0db306be55d9e03c6986121dbe1449a1f71b70df037c9dbd3a1fcad8"} {"question" "q-132587" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-132587" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=3x-2\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=3x-5\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=4x+3\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=6x+3\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=6x-5\\end{aligned}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-132587" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=6x-5\\end{aligned}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-132595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ Given that the point $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=-\\frac{2{x}^{2}+y}{{y}^{3}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-132595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(\\sqrt{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(\\sqrt{2},2) & =-\\frac{2(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+2}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4+2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\frac{3}{4}(y-2) \\\\ z & =-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-132595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ae308f1e0db306be55d9e03c6986121dbe1449a1f71b70df037c9dbd3a1fcad8"} {"question" "q-132595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-132595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{1}{4}y-2\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{2}y+1\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=\\frac{1}{2}y+2\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=\\frac{3}{2}y-1\\end{aligned}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-132595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-132601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,2,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}={x}^{2}y+2x-y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-132601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-2,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,2) & =(-2)^{2}(2)+2(-2)-(2) \\\\  & =8-4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,2) & ={f}_{y}(-2,2)(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =2(y-2) \\\\ z & =2y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2y-1$ (or $2y-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $j+2\\,k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-132601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ae308f1e0db306be55d9e03c6986121dbe1449a1f71b70df037c9dbd3a1fcad8"} {"question" "q-132601" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-132601" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\,j+3\\,k"] ["math" "-2\\,j+k"] ["math" "3\\,j+k"] ["math" "j+2\\,k"] ["math" "j-3\\,k"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-132601" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "j+2\\,k"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-132602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,7)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-132602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(0,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & ={0}^{2}+{1}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1) \\\\  & =0+1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(0,1) & ={f}_{x}(0,1)(x-0) \\\\ z-7 & =1(x) \\\\ z & =x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,7)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x+7$ (or $x-z+7=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-1⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-1⟩=⟨1,0,1⟩$ is also parallel to it." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-132602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ae308f1e0db306be55d9e03c6986121dbe1449a1f71b70df037c9dbd3a1fcad8"} {"question" "q-132602" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-132602" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-1,0,2⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,0,-1⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,0,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,0,2⟩"] ["math" "⟨2,0,1⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-132602" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨1,0,1⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/state.json" "file_sha256" "441468fb462c0e22b53500fdd42ba4f6cd4fd576e48100a15832e1f6b2f1431c"} {"question" "q-36219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053022/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "210e41d20bf93999c13a81d997d1e3a268f267ca4aa9fb59d74b218c92bf4b90" "reviewer_session" "01a11908-fa8d-7c61-8e03-c1185d0b9506" "rationale" "The intersection fixes x=1. At P(1,2,4), f_y(1,2)=(3+2)/sqrt(12+4)=5/4. Therefore its tangent line satisfies x=1 and z−4=(5/4)(y−2), equivalently z=(5/4)y+3/2. This is original option b and agrees with the authentic worked solution and stored key. Saved grading reports Correct. Both serialized systems preserve these two equations." "bindings" {"problem" "17b80ea1ee6a43682d1d8f9ffb1d864a228990afc00640b4839aa7b7c50e5627" "worked_solution" "07041fe7cb89b16d732089816317663cfba16e6052b93f264f300b7c675da26b" "original_field" "709858c797ed5266a2ddb1aa30f3e39997e4e74e3b4dcf162c28f9d3406a8fce" "reviewed_field" "084b70510f46b5d9a7200322fea5d92820762363eaad61ede3a4ab40fe12f70b" "raw_widget" "1c9ae7e6751ec471a17ac6438cc11b130636218b4d6c011251f799176e26e374"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-36219-before.png" "sha256" "9654f7c4a3825bf3c50010a8baf846597ab1128c2bb293fd9c317e46cd39b473"} {"path" "q-36219-before.json" "sha256" "e6c25eab3f5920966d0c1a390408dd3261c954a75c867e80e21301f179d36b20"} {"path" "history-q-36219.png" "sha256" "712476081dab09172cc92a6b428826a277db86d8603704c217b331e8989c3f5e"} {"path" "history-q-36219.json" "sha256" "e27216b391cd93bf9f7228422625c256c80c582116cf087cc384637923d2c339"} {"path" "q-36219-after.json" "sha256" "572c78ecf163c07f85b43059d94f7a3f01f9101fc451df09f7d1ee670a296e38"} {"path" "edb-import/questions.edn" "sha256" "2b0d0b0e8491f73901c00f98e6789c334bcc1e7c4fd6aa41d2154294b5b47ff7"}]} {"question" "q-36219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\begin{a